From f95904d528a7ea71b95a3e6a26bf16ab6c87534a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Braden <snufflyy@proton.me>
Date: Mon, 19 Oct 2026 09:12:10 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 3/3] spu_workload: the POSIX SPU task thread forgets its MFC
 slots too

The Win32 spu_async_thread drops its stack range from both the lock-line
reserver set and the MFC slot table on the way out. d2ff9fb brought the
first half to POSIX (the fork predates spu_mfc_forget_range); do the
second as well, so an SPU context that finished holding an MFC slot does
not leave the slot pointing into the unmapped thread stack.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 runtime/spu/spu_workload.c | 5 ++++-
 1 file changed, 4 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/runtime/spu/spu_workload.c b/runtime/spu/spu_workload.c
index c655ad7..793b760 100644
--- a/runtime/spu/spu_workload.c
+++ b/runtime/spu/spu_workload.c
@@ -832,7 +832,10 @@ static void* spu_async_thread(void* p)
       }
 #endif
       extern void spu_coh_forget_range(uintptr_t, uintptr_t);
-      if (hi > lo) spu_coh_forget_range(lo, hi); }
+      /* And from the MFC slot table, as the Win32 thread does: a context
+       * that finished holding an MFC slot would leave it pointing into the
+       * unmapped stack too. */
+      if (hi > lo) { spu_coh_forget_range(lo, hi); spu_mfc_forget_range(lo, hi); } }
     return NULL;
 }
 #endif
-- 
2.55.0

